pulse: show a day/night reading on cameras where the streamer has none

The status page asks the streamer for the isp_again metric and hides the
reading when it answers -1. On Ingenic it always answers -1: majestic never
fills its ISP metric store there, and /metrics/isp is empty. So on those
cameras the day/night value simply never appears - and the thresholds it
exists to help choose, minThreshold and maxThreshold, are left to be guessed
at.

The number does exist, in one of two places depending on what the camera has,
and which one is used matters: whatever is shown here has to be in the same
units as the thresholds the user then types in, or it is worse than showing
nothing at all.

A camera with a photoresistor reads its light from the ADC - which is what
isp.adcReadout says - and that is the number its thresholds are compared
against. One without reads the ISP's own analog gain, published in
/proc/jz/isp as AeAGain, the same quantity majestic's help text names for the
thresholds. Choosing between them by the same setting the switching itself
uses keeps the display and the decision on one scale.

Consulted only when the streamer returned nothing or -1, and the block is
guarded on /proc/jz/isp existing, so no other platform changes behaviour or
pays for the read.

Signed-off-by: Oskari Rauta <oskari.rauta@gmail.com>

A failed conversion also has to be filtered here. The driver lets one out
as 1047938 - a raw -145 wrapped through the unsigned scaling - mostly in
darkness, where the photoresistor's impedance is highest and the
conversion settles slowest. Showing that would be worse than showing
nothing, because it is precisely the number somebody would then choose
their thresholds against. Blanked, so the poll falls back to -1 and the
page hides the reading for that second.

Two further things the sensor forces.

A failed conversion escapes the driver as an enormous positive number -
1047938, a raw -145 carried through the unsigned scaling - and it is not
rare: seen for two seconds at a time and once for over a minute, at any
light level. It has nothing to do with the light: the reading jumps
straight there from mid-range and back, never climbing, so it is a failed
conversion rather than the scale overflowing. Answering it correctly means
replying with the previous good reading, which needs memory that a CGI
invoked afresh for every poll does not have.

So prefer the value nightsync publishes, which is already filtered that
way, and fall back to reading the sensor here when it is absent or stale -
a stopped daemon should not freeze the display on its last word. The
figure shown is then the same one the thresholds are actually compared
against, rather than a second opinion formed differently.

--- a/www/cgi-bin/j/pulse.cgi
+++ b/www/cgi-bin/j/pulse.cgi
@@ -6,6 +6,79 @@
 	daynight_value=$(wget -q -T1 localhost/metrics/isp?value=isp_again -O -)
 fi
 
+# On Ingenic the streamer never fills its ISP metric store, so isp_again answers
+# -1 and the day/night reading this page is built around never appears at all.
+# The number does exist, in one of two places, and showing the right one is the
+# whole point: it has to be in the units the thresholds are chosen in.
+#
+#   isp.adcReadout on   the camera has a photoresistor, its light level comes
+#                       from the ADC, and that is what the thresholds compare
+#                       against - so that is what belongs here.
+#   otherwise           the ISP's own analog gain, which is the quantity
+#                       minThreshold and maxThreshold are documented against.
+#
+# Both read higher as it gets darker, so the scale never reverses under the
+# reader. Consulted only when the streamer has nothing to say, and the whole
+# block is guarded on the proc entry existing, so no other platform changes
+# behaviour or pays for the extra read.
+# nightsync publishes the light level it is actually deciding on, already
+# filtered. Prefer it: the sensor can return a failed conversion as an enormous
+# number, and answering that correctly means replying with the previous good
+# reading - which needs memory this page does not have, being invoked afresh for
+# every poll. It also means the figure shown is the same one the thresholds are
+# compared against, rather than a second opinion.
+#
+# The published line is "<epoch> <value>". Anything older than a few polls is
+# treated as absent, so a stopped daemon falls through to reading the sensor
+# here instead of freezing the display on its last word.
+if [ -z "$daynight_value" ] || [ "$daynight_value" = "-1" ]; then
+	if [ -r /tmp/nightsync.light ]; then
+		read -r light_stamp light_value < /tmp/nightsync.light
+		case "${light_stamp}:${light_value}" in
+			''|*[!0-9:]*)
+				;;
+			*)
+				if [ $(( $(date +%s) - light_stamp )) -lt 10 ]; then
+					daynight_value=$light_value
+				fi
+				;;
+		esac
+	fi
+fi
+
+if [ -z "$daynight_value" ] || [ "$daynight_value" = "-1" ]; then
+	if [ -d /proc/jz/isp ]; then
+		if [ "$(cli -g .isp.adcReadout 2>/dev/null)" = "true" ] &&
+		   [ -r /dev/jz_adc_aux_0 ]; then
+			# A binary unsigned long rather than text, so od converts it.
+			# The device name is the one load_ingenic points at whichever
+			# AUX channel this board's sensor is on.
+			daynight_value=$(od -An -tu4 -N4 /dev/jz_adc_aux_0 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' \n')
+
+			# A failed conversion escapes the driver as an enormous
+			# positive number rather than as an error - 1047938, which
+			# is a raw -145 wrapped through the unsigned scaling. It
+			# turns up mostly in darkness, where the photoresistor's
+			# impedance is highest and the conversion settles slowest.
+			# Showing it would be worse than showing nothing: it is the
+			# number somebody would then set their thresholds against.
+			# Blank it and let this poll fall back to -1, which the page
+			# already knows to hide; the next poll a second later has a
+			# good value.
+			case "$daynight_value" in
+				''|*[!0-9]*)
+					daynight_value=""
+					;;
+				*)
+					[ "$daynight_value" -gt 100000 ] && daynight_value=""
+					;;
+			esac
+		else
+			daynight_value=$(awk '/^AeAGain /{print $3; exit}' /proc/jz/isp/* 2>/dev/null)
+		fi
+	fi
+fi
+
 if [ -n "$temp" ]; then
 	soc_temp="${temp%.*}°C"
 fi
